==English==

===Etymology===
From an alteration of {{m|en|rascallion}}, a fanciful elaboration of {{m|en|rascal|gloss=someone who is naughty}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɹæpˈskæljən/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-rapscallion.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic}} A [[rascal]], [[scamp]], [[rogue]], or [[scoundrel]].
#* {{RQ:Twain Huckleberry Finn|chapter=XXVIII|passage=“If I get away I sha’n’t be here,” I says, “to prove these '''rapscallions''' ain’t your uncles, and I couldn’t do it if I was here. I could swear they was beats and bummers, that’s all, though that’s worth something.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1901|author=w:Joseph Conrad; w:Ford Madox Ford|title=The Inheritors|chapter=3
|passage=She was the sister who had remained within the pale; I, the '''rapscallion''' of a brother whose vagaries were trying to his relations.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1982|author=w:Kurt Vonnegut|title=w:Deadeye Dick|chapter=1
|passage=She had a studio built for him on a loft of the carriage house behind the family mansion when he was only ten years old, and she hired a '''rapscallion''' German cabinetmaker, who had studied art in Berlin in his youth, to give Father drawing and painting lessons on weekends and after school.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en-US|author=Scott Heller|title=Stephen Fry Knows He’s Become a Middle-Aged Cliché|work=The New York Times|date=2025-06-19|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/06/19/books/review/stephen-fry-odyssey.html|issn=0362-4331|quotee=w:Stephen Fry|passage=But I’m the kind of '''rapscallion''' who often prefers the very good to the GREAT.}}

